39.body:
Nobody legitimate connects to telnet, mail, RDP or database ports on these servers, so one blocked attempt is proof of scanning. The defaults are 32 services neither server runs; each showed up 100-10,000 times in the Sep-Oct month of UFW blocks: [ports]
tripwire = [ "21/tcp", "23/tcp", "2323/tcp", # FTP, Telnet "25/tcp", "465/tcp", "587/tcp", # SMTP, SMTPS, submission (no MX points here) "110/tcp", "995/tcp", "143/tcp", "993/tcp", # POP3(S), IMAP(S) "135/tcp", "139/tcp", "445/tcp", "3389/tcp", "5985/tcp", "5986/tcp", # Windows RPC, NetBIOS, SMB, RDP, WinRM "5900/tcp", "5901/tcp", "1723/tcp", "7547/tcp", "8291/tcp", # VNC, PPTP, TR-069, MikroTik Winbox "389/tcp", "2375/tcp", "2376/tcp", # LDAP, Docker API "1433/tcp", "1521/tcp", "3306/tcp", "5432/tcp", "27017/tcp", # MS SQL, Oracle, MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB "6379/tcp", "9200/tcp", "11211/tcp", # Redis, Elasticsearch, Memcached
] Ports you serve can't trip. UFW only logs blocked packets, so a port that is open on the host never produces a tripwire event. Mail ports only while no mail is delivered here. example.org's MX records point to Proton, so no legitimate mail server connects to port 25. Take 25 out for a domain whose mail is meant to come to this host but isn't served yet, and for a domain without MX records whose A record is this host: mail servers then deliver to the A record (implicit MX), bounces of forged mail included. A running Postfix opens its ports and can't trip anyway. Defaults are TCP only. A UDP packet with a forged sender is trivial to send, and a forged tripwire hit bans an innocent address. TCP is no real protection either: UFW logs the very first SYN, before any handshake, and that SYN can carry any source address too. So a tripwire hit never bans an address that logged in within trust_ttl (see below), and bans never drop replies to the server's own connections ("Firewall backends"). Impact on the Sep–Oct month: 13,480 tripwire hits, mostly telnet, banned about 7,500 scanners for an hour on first contact, instead of letting them probe further. Port 23 alone was 9,807 of the month's 142k UFW blocks.
